      <h2 id="site-pages">1. The website itself</h2>
      <p>I design and build every page on your site from scratch, so the layout, words, and design fit your business. You get a home page, an about page, a services page (often with a section for each service), a service-area page, a contact page, and any other pages your business needs. The plan covers up to 10 content pages, and each page beyond that costs $100. Most service businesses are well served by six to eight, because a few thorough pages do more than many thin ones.</p>

      <h2 id="operational-pages">2. Operational and legal pages</h2>
      <p>Every site I launch also includes the pages a professional website needs: Privacy Policy, Terms of Use, Accessibility Statement, a sitemap page for visitors, Style Guide, Contact, and Thank-You. I write each one for your business; the style guide, for example, is a visual standard you can hand to a printer or a sign painter. These pages don't count against your page limit, and they cost nothing extra. Your site also gets a branded "page not found" page, icons for every device, and the preview image that appears when someone shares your link.</p>

      <h2 id="design">3. Design</h2>
      <p>Each site gets its own look, made for your trade. I choose the fonts, colors, photo style, layout, and buttons to match your business. Look at my <a href="/samples/" class="inline-link">samples page</a>: a roofing company looks different from a landscape designer, and a landscape designer looks different from an electrician. Every site also works in light and dark mode, and every color pairing is easy to read.</p>

      <h2 id="performance">4. Speed</h2>
      <p>Your site loads in under a second on a phone, which is faster than 95% of the websites you visit on a given day. Visitors feel the difference even if they never name it. Every page scores 95 to 100 on Google's speed test, on phones and on computers.</p>

      <h2 id="accessibility">5. Accessibility</h2>
      <p>Your site works for everyone, including people who use a screen reader, a keyboard instead of a mouse, magnification, voice control, or larger text. I build to the <span class="term" data-term="Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, version 2.2, Level AA. The W3C accessibility standard.">WCAG 2.2 AA</span> accessibility standard, which U.S. courts treat as the working minimum under the Americans with Disabilities Act. Before launch, I test every site with screen readers on Mac, iPhone, and Windows, with a keyboard alone, and zoomed to 200%.</p>

      <h2 id="mobile">6. Phones and tablets</h2>
      <p>I design your site for the phone first and then expand it for tablets and computers, because more than 70% of your visitors will arrive on a small screen. Tablets get their own attention, since they fall in a gap most sites ignore, and every button is large enough to tap easily.</p>

      <h2 id="seo">7. Showing up in Google</h2>
      <p>I build the foundation that makes Google willing to rank your site, and your rankings grow over time from the content you publish and the reviews your customers leave. Each page gets its own title and description with your service and city, plus labels that tell Google what kind of business you are. I also link related pages to each other and submit your site to Google at launch.</p>
      <p>I don't promise a specific ranking position, because no one controls Google's results. I do promise that your site's technical foundation will never be the thing that holds you back.</p>

      <h2 id="analytics">8. Visitor statistics</h2>
      <p>You can see how many people visit your site, which pages they read, and where they came from, and you get a private dashboard link on launch day. These statistics need no cookie banner, they're included in the plan, and I never sell or share the data. I don't add advertising trackers by default, because they slow the site and require a cookie banner. If you want Google Analytics, I can add it at your request, though I don't recommend it.</p>

      <h2 id="forms">9. Contact forms</h2>
      <p>When a visitor fills out a form on your site, the message lands in your inbox, with no separate dashboard to check. Spam filtering works without puzzles for your customers to solve. After sending, the visitor sees a thank-you page with next steps and gets an automatic email with your phone number. I never store form messages on my own systems; they live in your inbox.</p>
      <p>For trade-specific intake, I build forms that ask for the job details that matter: the address, scope, and urgency of a plumbing emergency, the roof's age and insurance carrier for roofing, or BTU and SEER specs for HVAC. See the <a href="/sample-forms/" class="inline-link">sample forms</a> for the eight industry templates I maintain.</p>

      <h2 id="search">10. Site search</h2>
      <p>Your visitors can search every page on your site. The search runs inside their own browser, so there's nothing extra for me to maintain or for you to pay for, and it doesn't track anyone.</p>

      <h2 id="hosting">11. Hosting and your domain</h2>
      <p>I host your site on a fast global network, so you never deal with a hosting company's control panel or bills. Over the last 12 months, uptime has been 99.99%. I check your site every minute from three regions, and I'm alerted to a problem before you'd notice it. Your domain stays registered in your name at your registrar. I point it at my hosting, and I never own a client's domain.</p>

      <h2 id="email">12. Email on your domain</h2>
      <p>You can have email at your own domain, such as <code>you@yourdomain.com</code>, at no extra setup cost. I don't host email, but I set up the records that make it work with the provider you choose: Google Workspace or Microsoft 365 (each $7.20 per user a month), or free forwarding to your existing Gmail. That setup is included at launch.</p>

      <h2 id="security">13. Security</h2>
      <p>There's almost nothing on your site to break into, because it has no admin login, no database, and no plugins. The secure padlock (HTTPS) and strict security settings are in place on day one. Your site's code is stored in a private, protected account, and I'm alerted whenever the code needs a security update.</p>

      <h2 id="backups">14. Backups</h2>
      <p>Every change I make to your site is saved as its own backup, so if something goes wrong, I can restore any earlier version in under five minutes.</p>

      <h2 id="updates">15. Content updates</h2>
      <p>Content updates are unlimited on the plan. When something changes, email or text me, or use the <a href="/content-update/" class="inline-link">content update form</a>; all three reach the same inbox. I handle routine edits, such as typos, hours, prices, photos, staff bios, or a single new service page, quickly on most business days. Larger jobs, like a new set of related pages or a fresh blog post, take a little longer, and I give you a timeline before I start. Work outside the plan, such as a custom calculator, an outside booking system, or an email-marketing template, costs $100 an hour, and I send a written estimate first.</p>

      <h2 id="emergencies">16. Emergency support</h2>
      <p>If your site goes down, wrong information goes live, or someone reports a security issue, call me directly, and I'll drop what I'm doing to fix it. Emergency support is included in every plan. It covers error pages, wrong contact details, a wrong price on a live promotion, suspicious form messages, and a defaced page. Call or text me first; the <a href="/emergency/" class="inline-link">emergency form</a> is the backup. Anything else, like a new page, a photo swap, or a wording change, is a regular content update.</p>

      <h2 id="blog">17. Blog (included)</h2>
      <p>The blog is part of the plan, and each new post counts as a content update, so it never becomes a separate charge. You write a post in plain text or a Google Doc, and I lay it out and publish it for you. Each post gets a table of contents, an author card with your photo, share buttons, and links to related posts.</p>

      <h2 id="service-areas">18. Service-area pages</h2>
      <p>If you serve several cities or neighborhoods, I build a separate page for each one, with its own local details, such as streets, ZIP codes, and landmarks, and links to nearby areas. These pages rank for local searches that a single services page can't reach. Adding a new area is a small change that goes live in about a minute, and I've built sites with more than 30 area pages.</p>

      <h2 id="review">19. What's not included</h2>
      <p>To save you the email later, here's what sits outside the plan. I can add anything in the first list at $100 an hour, and I don't take on the work in the second.</p>
      <p><strong>Available at $100 an hour, with a written estimate first:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Online stores.</strong> If you need to sell products with inventory, I connect a hosted store such as Shopify or Snipcart instead of building a custom cart.</li>
        <li><strong>User accounts and logins.</strong> These sit outside the plan, but I'm happy to quote a login area if you need one.</li>
        <li><strong>Live chat.</strong> I can add a chat service such as Tawk.to or Intercom, though I don't run it for you. Most service businesses do better closing jobs on the phone.</li>
        <li><strong>Email newsletters.</strong> I can connect a service such as Buttondown or Mailchimp, though I don't manage your email marketing.</li>
        <li><strong>Custom calculators, galleries, or video pages.</strong> I quote them up front and build them at the same hourly rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Moving data from an old platform.</strong> Whether it's content, redirects, or visitor history, the move is hourly, with a written estimate.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Things I don't take on:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ongoing SEO writing.</strong> I write and edit your site's pages, but I don't run a separate content-marketing program. If you draft posts, I publish them at no charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Ads or paid social media.</strong> I build the landing pages, but I don't run the ad accounts.</li>
        <li><strong>Photography or video.</strong> I can recommend a local photographer, but I don't shoot.</li>
        <li><strong>Logo design.</strong> I don't design logos. If you need one, I recommend <a href="https://looka.com/" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">Looka</a> (an AI logo maker, $20&ndash;$96 one time, fastest), <a href="https://99designs.com/" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">99designs</a> (a designer contest, $299 and up, the most variety), or <a href="https://www.fiverr.com/" rel="noopener" class="inline-link">Fiverr Pro</a> (selected freelancers, $50&ndash;$500). Send me the final SVG or vector file, and I'll add it to your site.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>If you want any of these and I know a specialist, I'll introduce you. I do not take referral kickbacks.</p>

